\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

A machine-learning predictor can stand in for scarce gold-standard labels when estimating a population quantity. Prediction-powered inference (PPI) combines a small labeled sample, a large unlabeled one and the predictor's outputs into a confidence interval that is valid whatever the predictor's quality, and PPI++ tunes a scalar weight on the predictor to make the interval as short as the linear use of the predictor allows \citep{angelopoulos2023ppi,angelopoulos2023ppipp}. The width of the interval is what the analyst pays for with labels, so the practical question after validity is how much shorter it can be.

We study that question in its cleanest form: estimate a mean $\E[Y]$ with $n$ labels, $N$ unlabeled records and a fixed predictor $f$, none of which the analyst may change. What remains free is how the labels build the control variate $g(Z)$ and how the interval is formed. Classical missing-data and semi-supervised theory says that the best control variate is the regression function $\E[Y\mid Z]$, and gives its variance as a floor \citep{robins1994estimation,tsiatis2006semiparametric,zhang2019semi}. That floor does not say what to expect on a given dataset: the regression function must be learned from the same labels, and learning costs width. We ask three things. How far is PPI++ from the floor, exactly? When does learning a calibrated control variate from the labels pay for itself? And on the datasets practitioners use, how much room is there, where is there none, and where does every interval fail?

\paragraph{Contributions.} We claim no new efficiency principle; the floor and the cross-fitted estimator we use to reach it are classical \citep{robins1994estimation,chernozhukov2018double,gronsbell2022efficient}. We contribute:
\begin{itemize}
\item An exact expression for the gap between PPI++ and the floor, $(1/n-1/M)\Delta$ with $\Delta$ the departure of $\E[Y\mid f]$ from a line, which is zero if and only if $\E[Y\mid f]$ is affine; the extra term from the clip that \texttt{ppi-python} applies to the weight; and a break-even label count $n^\ast$ above which a learned control variate shortens the interval (\cref{sec:setting}).
\item A measurement of the headroom on the nine standard \texttt{ppi-python} datasets and a synthetic suite with known truth, with pre-fixed margins, paired width ratios against PPI++ and coverage in every cell (\cref{sec:experiments,sec:results}).
\item A test of the break-even formula, scored only where the measured crossing is bracketed by the sample sizes we ran, and a map of where intervals fail: labeling that depends on the data, distribution shift, rare events and small $n$.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{What we find.} On the nine datasets, at the reference $n$ ($500$; $200$ on the smallest pool) the best interval we construct is within $1\%$ of the width of the strongest valid PPI++ baseline (the package's, or one with its weight unclipped; label saving at most $1.1\%$), and an estimated floor is at most $3.3\%$ shorter than the package's interval, so for these predictors and sizes there is almost no room beyond PPI++. The one real cell that passes our gain rule against the package, \texttt{gene\_expression} at $n=100$ ($13\%$ of the labels), comes from the package's clip of its tuning weight, not from calibration. With $n\le100$ the learned control variate costs width (median $1.9\%$ above that baseline). Room is large only when $\E[Y\mid f]$ is far from affine (up to $47\%$ of the labels on synthetic populations), where the break-even formula locates the crossing. Side information and the interval construction add nothing on these datasets.
